Let $O$ be the center of $\cal C$, and let $P$ be the center of one of the smaller circles.
Draw radii $\overline{OP}$, $\overline{OQ}$, and $\overline{OR}$.
Triangle $POQ$ is a $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle,
so $OQ = OP + r = R - r$.
Triangle $POR$ is also a $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle,
so $OR = OP + 2r = R$.

The area of $\cal C$ is $\pi R^2 = 900\pi$,
and the combined area of the six smaller circles is $6\pi r^2 = 600\pi$.
Therefore, the area of the region inside $\cal C$ and outside all of the six circles in the ring is
\[K = 900\pi - 600\pi = 300\pi.\]
Since $\pi \approx 3.14159$, we have $\lfloor K\rfloor = \lfloor 300\pi\rfloor = \boxed{942}$.
The answer is 942

