Find the ratio of the area of $\triangle BCX$ to the area of $\triangle ACX$ in the diagram if $CX$ bisects $\angle ACB$. Express your answer as a common fraction. [asy]
import markers;

real t=27/(27+30);
pair A=(-15.57,0);
pair B=(8.43,0);
pair C=(0,25.65);
pair X=t*A+(1-t)*B;

draw(C--A--B--C--X);

label("$A$",A,SW);
label("$B$",B,E);
label("$C$",C,N);
label("$X$",X,NE);

//markangle(n=1,radius=15,A,C,X,marker(markinterval(stickframe(n=1),true)));
//markangle(n=1,radius=15,X,C,B,marker(markinterval(stickframe(n=1),true)));

label("$24$",.5*(B+A),S);
label("$27$",.5*(B+C),NE);
label("$30$",.5*(A+C),NW);
[/asy]

solution:
Since $CX$ bisects $\angle ACB$, we can apply the angle bisector theorem to get $\frac{AX}{BX} = \frac{AC}{BC}$. Plugging in the given lengths, we have $\frac{x}{y} = \frac{30}{27} = \frac{10}{9}$. 

We want to find the ratio of the area of $\triangle BCX$ to the area of $\triangle ACX$. The ratio of their bases is $y:x = 1:\frac{10}{9}$. The ratio of their heights is $BC:AC = 27:30 = 9:10$. 

Therefore, the ratio of their areas is $\left(\frac{1}{\frac{10}{9}}\right) \times \left(\frac{9}{10}\right) = \boxed{\frac{9}{10}}$.The answer is \frac{9}{10}

