solution:
Since $AB = BC = CD = DE = 4,$ the angles of the pentagon are all congruent, so let $\angle B = \angle C = \angle D = \angle E = \theta.$

[asy]
unitsize(1 cm);

pair A, B, C, D, E;

A = dir(90);
B = dir(90 - 72);
C = dir(90 - 2*72);
D = dir(90 - 3*72);
E = dir(90 - 4*72);

draw(Circle((0,0),1));
draw(A--B--C--D--E--cycle);

label("$A$", A, N);
label("$B$", B, NE);
label("$C$", C, SE);
label("$D$", D, SW);
label("$E$", E, NW);
[/asy]

Then
\begin{align*}
\cos \theta &= \frac{2 \cdot 4^2 - 1}{2 \cdot 4^2} = \frac{31}{32}, \\
\cos \theta &= \frac{2 \cdot 1^2 - 1}{2 \cdot 1^2} = \frac{1}{2}.
\end{align*}Therefore,
\[(1 - \cos \theta)(1 - \cos \theta) = \left( 1 - \frac{31}{32} \right) \left( 1 - \frac{1}{2} \right) = \frac{1}{32} \cdot \frac{1}{2} = \boxed{\frac{1}{64}}.\]The answer is \frac{1}{64}

