Find the value of $a$ so that the lines with the given equations are perpendicular. \begin{align*}
y &= 2x+13 \\
6y+ax &= 6.
\end{align*}

solution:
First, we plot the three points on a coordinate plane.
[asy]
unitsize(0.5cm);
pair A, B, C;
A = (4,-1);
B = (10,3);
C = (4,5);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);
label("$(4,-1)$", A, SW);
label("$(10,3)$", B, NE);
label("$(4,5)$", C, NW);
dot(A);
dot(B);
dot(C);
[/asy]
We can see that the triangle is a right triangle, with the right angle at $(4,-1)$.
The base of the triangle is the distance between $(4,-1)$ and $(10,3)$, which is $10-4=6$ units.
The height of the triangle is the distance between $(4,-1)$ and $(4,5)$, which is $5-(-1)=6$ units.
Therefore, the area of the triangle is $(6)(6)/2=\boxed{18}$ square units.
The answer is 18

