The inequalities $|x - 3| \le y \le 4 - |x - 1|$ represent two sets of inequalities:
\[x - 3 \le y \le 4 - x + 1\]and\[-(x - 3) \le y \le 4 - (1 - x).\]Simplifying, we have $x - y \le 3$ and $y \le x + 3$, as well as $y \ge x - 3$ and $-y \le x - 3$.

Plotting these four inequalities on the coordinate plane, we obtain the shaded region below.

[asy]
unitsize(0.5 cm);

fill((1,-3)--(3,0)--(3,3)--(1,6)--(-1,6)--(-3,3)--(-3,0)--(-1,-3)--cycle,gray(0.7));
draw((-3,0)--(3,0));
draw((0,-3)--(0,6));
draw((1,-3)--(3,0)--(3,3)--(1,6));
draw((-1,-3)--(-3,0)--(-3,3)--(-1,6));

label("$y = x + 3$", (3,6), NE);
label("$y = x - 3$", (-3,3), W);
label("$x - y = 3$", (3,-3), E);
label("$x - y \le 3$", (3/2,-1/2), NE);
label("$y \le x + 3$", (1/2,3/2), N);
[/asy]

The shaded region is symmetric about the line $y = x$, so we can find the area of one triangle and multiply by 2.  The area of the triangle with vertices $(1,-3)$, $(3,0)$, and $(3,3)$ is
\[\frac{1}{2} \cdot 2 \cdot 3 = 3,\]so the area of the shaded region is $2 \cdot 3 = \boxed{6}$ square units.The answer is 6

