solution:
To convert $8/11$ to a decimal, we perform long division. The remainder after each division is the numerator of the next division, so we have\[
\begin{array}{c|cccccccccc}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{0.} & 7 & 2 & 7 & 2 & 7 & 2 & \ldots \\
\cline{2-11}
11 & 8 & . & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & \ldots \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{8} & & 8 & & 8 & & 8 & \\
\cline{2-4}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & 2 & 0 & & & & & \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & 1 & 1 & & & & & \\
\cline{3-5}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 8 & 0 & & & & \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 7 & 7 & & & & \\
\cline{4-6}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & & 3 & 0 & & & \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & & 2 & 7 & & & \\
\cline{5-7}
\end{array}
\]Since we have a remainder of 3 after the first division, and a remainder of 30 after the second division, the repeating block starts after two decimal places.
Thus, the least number of digits in a repeating block is $\boxed{2}$.
The answer is 2

Using the product-to-sum identities, we can rewrite the expression as
\begin{align*}
\cos \theta_1 \sin \theta_2 + \cos \theta_2 \sin \theta_3 + \cos \theta_3 \sin \theta_4 + \cos \theta_4 \sin \theta_5 + \cos \theta_5 \sin \theta_1 &= \frac{1}{2}(\sin(\theta_1+\theta_2) + \sin(\theta_2+\theta_3) + \sin(\theta_3+\theta_4) + \sin(\theta_4+\theta_5) + \sin(\theta_5+\theta_1)) \\
&= \frac{1}{2}(\sin(\theta_1+\theta_2) + \sin(\theta_2+\theta_3) + \sin(\theta_3+\theta_4) + \sin(\theta_4+\theta_5) + \sin(\theta_1+\theta_5)).
\end{align*}
By the Triangle Inequality, each term in the expression is at most 1, so the maximum value is $\frac{1}{2}(1+1+1+1+1) = \boxed{\frac{5}{2}}.$
The answer is \frac{5}{2}

