draw(C--D);
draw(C--M);

label("$A$", A, SW);
label("$B$", B, SE);
label("$C$", C, N);
label("$D$", D, S);
label("$M$", M, S);
[/asy]

Since $\triangle ABC$ is isosceles, we have $AC = BC$, so $CD$ is the altitude to the hypotenuse of a $45^\circ$-$45^\circ$-$90^\circ$ triangle.
Since $AC = BC$, $M$ is the midpoint of $\overline{AB}$, so $MD = MA = MB$.

Therefore, $\triangle CDM$ is a $45^\circ$-$45^\circ$-$90^\circ$ triangle, and $MD = 10$ units.

In a $45^\circ$-$45^\circ$-$90^\circ$ triangle, the length of the hypotenuse is $\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg.
So $CD = MD\sqrt{2} = 10\sqrt{2}$ units.

Since $CD$ is the altitude of $\triangle ABC$ from the right angle, $CD$ is also the length of a leg of $\triangle ABC$.

Therefore, the length of a leg of $\triangle ABC$ is $\boxed{10\sqrt{2}}$ units.The answer is 10\sqrt{2}

