Let $z = x + yi,$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers.
We are given that $|x + yi - 5 - i| = 5,$ so
\[|(x - 5) + (y - 1)i| = 5.\]This equation represents a circle centered at $5 + i$ with radius 5.
Similarly, $|x + yi - 1 + 2i| = |(x - 1) + (y + 2)i|$ represents a circle centered at $1 - 2i$ with radius 5,
and $|x + yi - 9 - 4i| = |(x - 9) + (y + 4)i|$ represents a circle centered at $9 + 4i$ with radius 5.

[asy]
unitsize(0.4 cm);

pair A, B, C, D;

A = (5,1);
B = (1,2);
C = (9,-4);

draw(Circle(A,5));
draw(Circle(B,5));
draw(Circle(C,5));
draw((-5,0)--(15,0));
draw((0,-10)--(0,10));

dot("$5 + i$", A, E);
dot("$1 - 2i$", B, NW);
dot("$9 + 4i$", C, NE);
[/asy]

Note that the distance between the centers of the circles centered at $5 + i$ and $1 - 2i$ is $|4 + 3i| = 5$.
Therefore, the circles centered at $5 + i$ and $1 - 2i$ are tangent to each other.

[asy]
unitsize(0.4 cm);

pair A, B, C, D;

A = (5,1);
B = (1,2);
C = (9,-4);
D = (5,6);

draw(Circle(A,5));
draw(Circle(B,5));
draw(Circle(C,5));
draw((-5,0)--(15,0));
draw((0,-10)--(0,10));
draw((5,1)--(D),dashed);

dot("$5 + i$", A, E);
dot("$1 - 2i$", B, NW);
dot("$9 + 4i$", C, NE);
dot(D);
[/asy]

Also, the distance between the centers of the circles centered at $5 + i$ and $9 + 4i$ is $|4 - 3i| = 5$.
Therefore, the circles centered at $5 + i$ and $9 + 4i$ are also tangent to each other.

[asy]
unitsize(0.4 cm);

pair A, B, C, D;

A = (5,1);
B = (1,2);
C = (9,-4);
D = (5,-3);

draw(Circle(A,5));
draw(Circle(B,5));
draw(Circle(C,5));
draw((-5,0)--(15,0));
draw((0,-10)--(0,10));
draw((5,1)--(D),dashed);

dot("$5 + i$", A, E);
dot("$1 - 2i$", B, NW);
dot("$9 + 4i$", C, NE);
dot(D);
[/asy]

Therefore, the minimum value of $|z - 1 + 2i|^2 + |z - 9 - 4i|^2$ is $(5 + 5)^2 = \boxed{100}.$The answer is 100

