The equation of the ellipse shown below can be written as
\[\frac{(x - h)^2}{a^2} + \frac{(y - k)^2}{b^2} = 1.\]Find $h + k + a + b.$

[asy]
unitsize(0.3 cm);

int i, n = 10;

for (i = -n; i <= n; ++i) {
  draw((i,-n)--(i,n),gray(0.7));
  draw((-n,i)--(n,i),gray(0.7));
}

draw((0,-n)--(0,n));
draw((-n,0)--(n,0));

draw(shift((-4,2))*xscale(5)*yscale(3)*Circle((0,0),1),red);

dot((-4,2));
[/asy]

solution:
By Cauchy-Schwarz,
\[(x_1^2 + x_2^2 + x_3^2)(1^2 + 2^2 + 3^2) \ge (x_1 + 2x_2 + 3x_3)^2 = 60^2,\]so
\[x_1^2 + x_2^2 + x_3^2 \ge \frac{60^2}{1^2 + 2^2 + 3^2} = \frac{60^2}{14} = \frac{1800}{7}.\]Equality occurs when $\frac{x_1}{1} = \frac{x_2}{2} = \frac{x_3}{3},$ or $x_1 = 10,$ $x_2 = 20,$ and $x_3 = 30.$  Therefore, the smallest possible value is $\boxed{\frac{1800}{7}}.$The answer is \frac{1800}{7}

