We can write
\[\left| \frac{\beta - \alpha}{1 - \overline{\alpha} \beta} \right| = \frac{|\beta - \alpha|}{|1 - \overline{\alpha} \beta|}.\]Since $|\beta| = 1,$ $|\beta - \alpha|$ is the distance between $\alpha$ and $\beta$ in the complex plane.  Let $P$ and $Q$ be the points corresponding to $\alpha$ and $\beta,$ respectively.  Then $|PQ| = |\beta - \alpha|$:

[asy]
unitsize(1 cm);

pair P, Q;

P = (1,0.6);
Q = (0.4,0.9);

draw(Circle((0,0),1));
draw((-1.2,0)--(1.2,0));
draw((0,-1.2)--(0,1.2));
draw(P--Q,dashed);

dot("$P$", P, E);
dot("$Q$", Q, NW);
[/asy]

Thus, we want to maximize
\[\frac{|\beta - \alpha|}{|1 - \overline{\alpha} \beta|} = \frac{|PQ|}{|1 - \overline{P} Q|}.\]By the Triangle Inequality,
\[|PQ + 1 - \overline{P} Q| \ge |PQ| - |1 - \overline{P} Q|.\]Then
\[|1 - \overline{P} Q| \le |PQ| - |PQ + 1 - \overline{P} Q| = 1,\]so
\[\frac{|PQ|}{|1 - \overline{P} Q|} \le |PQ|.\]Equality occurs when $Q = -1,$ so the maximum value is $\boxed{1}.$

[asy]
unitsize(1 cm);

pair P, Q;

P = (1,0.6);
Q = (-1,0);

draw(Circle((0,0),1));
draw((-1.2,0)--(1.2,0));
draw((0,-1.2)--(0,1.2));
draw(P--Q,dashed);

dot("$P$", P, E);
dot("$Q$", Q, W);
[/asy]The answer is 1

